# SOLSHADE: SURVIVOR CLASS — Disaster-Relief Jumpsuit
### BEKW-CODE Spec | One-Piece Survival Garment, Four-Size Class System

---

## 1. Concept

A one-piece survival jumpsuit for mass disaster distribution — not a fashion garment, a wearable survival kit. Extends the SOLSHADE Wearable Line's power/climate platform (heat, active cooling, PV charging, waterproofing) into a single integrated suit pre-equipped with the tools a person actually needs to survive the first 72+ hours after a disaster and start recovering beyond it: water filtration, signaling, basic medical, cordage, light.

**Sizing philosophy:** four classes, not standard S/M/L/XL apparel sizing — this is built around real-world body variance in a mass-distribution context (a suit that only fits "average" body types fails a large share of the people who need it most).

---

## 2. Size Class System

| Class | Target Fit | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| **Small** | Children and petite adults | Scaled proportionally, not just shortened — pocket/kit placement adjusted so tool weight distribution stays comfortable on a smaller frame, not just a shrunk adult suit |
| **Medium** | Average adult build | Baseline pattern |
| **Large** | Larger adult build (~6ft / 200lb reference point) | Extended torso/limb length, standard kit loadout |
| **Big Bubba** | Oversized build — wide shoulders, large forearms/thighs relative to height, non-"tall" oversizing | Not just "Large scaled up" — cut for width/girth independent of height, since real body variance (a person with forearms bigger than another person's thighs) doesn't scale linearly from a height-based sizing chart |

Each class gets its own pattern, not a single pattern scaled by a multiplier — this matters specifically for the Big Bubba class, where height-based scaling would produce a suit that's too long and still too tight, which is the exact failure mode of most "one size fits most" disaster gear.

---

## 3. Base Garment (All Classes)

Inherits SOLSHADE Wearable Line architecture (Sections 3–4 of that spec) at jumpsuit scale:
- Waterproof-breathable outer shell, PV cells across shoulders/back/upper legs (max sun exposure zones, least joint-flex interference)
- Hybrid heat (resistive wire) / cool (fan-driven channel) climate layer, zoned at core/collar rather than full-body for power budget reasons
- Shared battery bank, PV-charged with wall-power topoff capability where available
- Full waterproof sealing at every seam and pocket junction

---

## 4. Integrated Survival Systems

**Water:**
- Built-in hollow-fiber membrane straw filter (LifeStraw-class filtration — removes bacteria/protozoa, not a substitute for treating known chemical contamination) integrated into a chest-mounted drinking tube, routed to an accessible intake point so the wearer can filter-drink from any found water source hands-free
- Collapsible water reservoir/bladder pocket, low-profile, doesn't interfere with mobility

**Medical:**
- Compact trauma kit integrated into a dedicated sealed pocket (tourniquet, compression bandage, basic wound care) — positioned for one-handed self-access (same principle as a seatbelt-cutter placement on a life vest, must be reachable under stress/injury)
- Emergency blanket (mylar-style) built into a lower-back pocket, doubles as signaling reflector

**Signaling:**
- High-visibility panel, concealable (zippered flap) so it's not permanently exposed if the wearer doesn't want to be visually flagged at all times, but deployable in seconds
- Integrated whistle, physically attached (not a loose accessory that gets lost)
- Small signal mirror in a hip pocket

**Light:**
- Low-power LED strip along the shoulder/collar line, run off the shared battery bank — hands-free lighting for nighttime movement or being located
- Manual crank backup on a chest-mounted micro-light for zero-battery scenarios (matches AIRFOLD's fail-safe manual-override principle)

**Utility:**
- Integrated cordage (paracord) woven into a beltline or seam, pull-out length available without needing a separate coil
- Multi-tool pocket, sized per class (Big Bubba class gets a wider tool pocket sized to actual hand/tool-grip proportions, not just a longer one)
- Fire-starting basics (ferro rod + tinder, sealed pocket) — low cost, high value, doesn't depend on battery power at all

---

## 5. Design Principles Carried From Base BEKW-CODE Covenants

1. **Redundant power paths for every critical function** — light and any active climate function has a non-electric fallback, consistent with AIRFOLD RELIEF's manual-override principle
2. **Field-serviceable** — pocket/kit contents replaceable/restockable in the field, not sealed-for-life
3. **No single point of failure on survival-critical items** — water filtration and fire-starting specifically do not depend on the garment's battery system at all

---

## 6. Relationship to Existing BEKW-CODE Canon

- Direct wearable-scale sibling to **AIRFOLD: RELIEF Class** — RELIEF is the shelter-and-cargo unit, SURVIVOR is the individual-wearable unit; a full disaster response deploys both together (RELIEF shelter units + individually distributed SURVIVOR jumpsuits per person)
- Inherits **SOLSHADE Wearable Line** power/climate architecture directly
- Shares **Bloomkit**'s "give tools, not just aid" inversion principle at the individual-body scale, same lineage as RELIEF at shelter scale

---

## 7. Open Items / Next BOM Pass

- Big Bubba class pattern needs real body-scan/anthropometric data rather than an assumed proportion set — this is the class most likely to be gotten wrong by guessing, and it's the class where getting it wrong matters most (that's the population most underserved by existing disaster-relief clothing sizing)
- Battery bank sizing for jumpsuit-scale PV area (legs + torso + shoulders) not yet calculated — likely higher harvest potential than the jacket-only Wearable Line given more surface area, worth recalculating rather than assuming jacket numbers scale up directly
- Kit loadout weight budget per class not yet set — Small/children's class needs a real weight ceiling so the kit doesn't become a burden rather than a help
- Water filter membrane replacement/service interval needs sourcing research (hollow-fiber filters have a finite lifespan before replacement, needs a real number for field-service planning)
